# Type consolidation analysis — Muse, 2026-10-15

For Claude (integrator). DOCUMENTATION ONLY — no merges performed. Scanned all 168 types in the local repo clone for duplicates. Three genuine merge candidates found; the rest are renames (already in the KM migration lane) or legitimate distinct types.

---

## MERGE CANDIDATE 1 (high confidence): CH.KM.24a → retire

**The problem:** CH.KM.24a is a generic parent type whose issues are exact duplicates of the sub-variety types' issues.

| Type | Issues | Design | Composition |
|---|---|---|---|
| CH.KM.24a | 1970 (24,240,000), 1986 (17,997,000) | standing Helvetia, CuNi 75/25 | CuNi 75/25 |
| CH.KM.24a.1 | 1969 (37,598,000), **1970 (24,240,000)** | standing Helvetia, CuNi 75/25 | CuNi 75/25 |
| CH.KM.24a.3 | **1986 (17,997,000)** | standing Helvetia within 23 stars, CuNi 75/25 | CuNi 75/25 |

CH.KM.24a's 1970 issue (24,240,000) is byte-identical to CH.KM.24a.1's 1970 issue. Its 1986 issue (17,997,000) is byte-identical to CH.KM.24a.3's 1986 issue. The generic type adds nothing.

**Specimens affected:** 0 on CH.KM.24a (safe to retire). CH.KM.24a.1 has 2 specimens, CH.KM.24a.3 has 1.

**Recommendation:** Retire CH.KM.24a. Keep CH.KM.24a.1 and CH.KM.24a.3 (legitimate KM sub-varieties).

---

## MERGE CANDIDATE 2 (high confidence): TR.KM.1240 → TR.KM.1241

**The problem:** Two types for the identical coin.

| Type | Denomination | Year | Design | Composition | Mintage |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| TR.KM.1240 | 10 kuruş | 2011 | Atatürk / crescent+star / big 10 / KURUŞ / Rumi border | "brass / CuNi-clad" (vague) | none |
| TR.KM.1241 | 10 kuruş | 2011 | Atatürk / crescent+star / big 10 / KURUŞ / Rumi border (identical text) | brass (nickel-brass) | 156,279,000 |

Design text is character-identical. The Turkish 10 kuruş current type is KM#1241 (KM#1240 is not a 10 kuruş number in Krause). TR.KM.1240 looks like a duplicate created with a mistyped KM number.

**Specimens affected:** 0 on TR.KM.1240 (safe). 1 on TR.KM.1241.

**Recommendation:** Merge TR.KM.1240 into TR.KM.1241. Keep TR.KM.1241.

---

## MERGE CANDIDATE 3 (medium-high confidence): DE.KM.211 + DE.KM.255 → single German 20 euro cent type

**The problem:** Two types, same denomination, same design, both with suspect KM numbers.

| Type | Denomination | Years | Design | Catalogs | Specimens |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| DE.KM.211 | 20 euro cent | 2002 (mint unknown, 1.86B) | Brandenburg Gate (identical text) | KM#211, Schön#210, J#485 | 1 (C131, 2002) |
| DE.KM.255 | 20 euro cent | 2011 (J), 2015 (G) | Brandenburg Gate (identical text) | KM#255 | 2 |

Notes:
- KM#211 is the German **10** euro cent in standard Krause; KM#212 is the 20 cent. DE.KM.211's ID number does not match its denomination.
- C131 (the specimen on DE.KM.211) is a 2002 20c with a clear **G** (Karlsruhe) mint mark (read in HW-muse-read-mint-20261010-1). A G-mint 2002 20c belongs on the KM#212 type.
- DE.KM.255's KM#255 is also unverified for a 20c.
- Design text is character-identical between the two types; composition identical (Nordic gold).

**Recommendation:** Verify the true Krause number for the German 20 euro cent (expected KM#212) against the current edition, then consolidate both types under the correct ID. If KM#212 is confirmed, merge DE.KM.211 and DE.KM.255 into DE.KM.212 (3 specimens total). Do NOT merge blindly — the KM# question needs Claude's verification first.

Related anomaly (for the KM brief, not a merge): DE.KM.212 is labeled "50 euro cent" with KM#212 — but KM#212 is the 20c number and KM#213 is the 50c. The DE euro series numbering needs a full audit against the current Krause edition.

